# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# CYCLE 1 / ATTEMPT 3 on laravel-framework, TWO-STEP PROBE, RE-QUESTION
# (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md). TWO steps, names verbatim and in the order
# of scenarios/laravel-framework.yaml (the judge pairs by name).
#
# WHAT CHANGED FROM ATTEMPTS 1 AND 2, AND WHY THE RUBRIC HAD TO CHANGE WITH IT
# Attempt 1 scored CALLS (baseline 0.9375) and attempt 2 scored HOLDS (baseline 0.8333). Both
# relations are written in PHP as a literal token on the scored line - `->method(` in the
# first, `protected Type $x` / `$this->x = $x` in the second - so both times the location came
# free with the search that found the file. This draft scores neither. It scores REACHING
# THROUGH AN INTERMEDIATE: the routine holds nothing, calls nothing, and the object sits one
# or two declarations further along in a file the routine never mentions.
#
# WHY map_quality carries the most weight: the answer is a set of ROUTES, and a route without
# its middle is not an answer to this ticket - the middle is the thing that gets re-plumbed.
# Four of the twelve scored routes are a field declaring a narrow promise whose only concrete
# satisfier is the holder; four are a call to a held collaborator; two are a container short
# string resolved at wiring time; two are a bare `extends`, where the routine's own file
# declares nothing at all. An answer that lists endpoints without middles has restated the
# question.
#
# WHY specificity demands the starting line AND every intermediate AND the count AND the
# failure: the failure this criterion exists to catch is a run of routes named together under
# one shared reason with no location on any of them. It also catches the inverse of attempts 1
# and 2: a line where the object is HELD, or a line where one of its routines is CALLED, is
# the middle of somebody else's route and was pinned in step 1 - offering it here is answering
# the two questions that already failed on this repo.
#
# WHY step 1 asks for the direct holders explicitly: they are the middles, both arms will
# reach them, and they do not score. Naming them in step 1 is deliberate - it puts the
# discriminator on the far side of them rather than on them. What step 1 does NOT ask for is
# the roll of the far side: on the neighbouring repo in this vertical, asking step 1 for the
# enumerated working ground moved BOTH arms together, because the plain arm wrote its covering
# sweep in step 1 and re-read the same output in step 2.
audience: |
  An AI coding agent that will use this answer to replace the object every part of this
  framework is handed in order to read and write files on the local disk. Today that object is
  a plain synchronous wrapper: one instance is made at boot, registered under a short name,
  handed to whatever asks, every routine blocks until the disk answers, and a failure comes
  back as an exception thrown where the call was made. After the change the work happens off
  the calling thread and a failure surfaces where the work is awaited, not where it was
  requested. Score for that agent, not a human reader and not a docs page.
  A perfect answer leaves the agent able to make that change knowing two things. First, what
  the object is today: what it does grouped by job, where the single shared instance is made
  and under what short name it is registered, how a caller that never names the type still
  ends up holding it, where a disk failure surfaces today, and which collaborators hold one
  directly - each located at file:line. Second, and this is what decides the answer: every
  route by which code that holds none of them still ends up doing this work, with the thing in
  the middle named.
  A route is not a place. It has an end - a routine that will be doing local file work through
  the new object - and it has a middle, which is what the ticket has to re-plumb. A place that
  is handed the object and works it directly is NOT an answer here: that place is the middle
  of somebody else's route, it belongs to the first part, and naming it again earns nothing.
  What separates a good answer from a long one is the middle, and the middles are of four
  different kinds. Some routines call a collaborator they hold, and the collaborator is the
  holder. Some inherit the work from a class they extend, so the field is declared in a file
  they never open and their own file declares nothing to find. Some write a narrow promise on
  a field - a store, a loader, a finder - and the only concrete thing in the framework
  satisfying that promise is itself a holder, so the file names an idea rather than a type
  that leads anywhere. And some are linked at wiring time out of a short string, so no type is
  named on either side and no declaration anywhere connects them. Routes run to more than one
  link, and the count differs by row.
  Most of this layer sits in parts of the framework whose own subject has nothing to do with
  disks or files, and in ten of the twelve routes that matter the routine's own file contains
  no occurrence of the object's name at all. The part of the tree obviously about file access
  holds the object itself and the collaborators built out of it; an answer dense there and
  thin elsewhere has enumerated inside one search scope and mistaken it for the framework.
  Every route must be pinned to the exact file:line of the routine that starts it with that
  routine named, with every intermediate between there and the object named at its own
  file:line, with how many links that is, and with one phrase on what goes wrong for that
  routine once the work no longer blocks and the failure no longer surfaces where it was
  requested.

steps:
- name: Map the local file access contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        Is the object presented as one connected picture - what it does grouped by job, where
        the single shared instance is made, the short name it is registered under, how a
        caller that never names the type still ends up holding it, and where a disk failure
        surfaces today - rather than as a list of routines or a roll of places the name
        appears? The registration under a short name and the failure path are the two that
        matter most, because between them they are why so much of this framework can depend on
        the object without ever naming it, and why the change is dangerous. An answer that
        locates both and says so has understood the ticket.
        The step then asks for the DIRECT HOLDERS: the small set of objects handed the
        instance that keep it in a field of their own. Are they named, each pinned to the
        field or the constructor that fills it, and does each carry a phrase on what it does
        with the handle? This part is deliberately the easy part and it is asked for on
        purpose - it is the middle of the picture and the next step is about the far side of
        it - so score coverage and location here, not difficulty.
        The step finally asks for a deliberately SMALL third thing: the KINDS of indirect
        route, one example each. Score whether the kinds are genuinely distinct and correctly
        characterised. Do NOT reward an exhaustive enumeration of the indirect places
        themselves: it was not asked for, and a long located list is not a better answer here
        than four accurate kinds. Could the next step act from this orientation with targeted
        lookups rather than a fresh exploration?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every place the answer names carry an exact file:line AND the name of the routine
        or class it lives in? A bare filename is not specific. A file:line with nothing named
        around it is not specific - the maintainer asked whose code it is, not where the
        character offset falls. Where the answer names the construction of the shared
        instance, the short name it is registered under, or a collaborator's field, is the
        line that actually constructs, registers or declares located, and not just asserted
        from a class name or a familiar convention?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Does each part carry one phrase on what it contributes - what the registration buys a
        caller that has only a name, what each direct holder actually does with its handle,
        where a refusal from the disk lands today - and does the answer connect those facts to
        why code far away can depend on this object without naming it, rather than restating
        the list of routines?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer separate what it verified by reading from what it inferred from a name
        or a familiar framework convention? This matters more than usual here: closed-book,
        this model can name 46 of the object's 54 members, so its surface invites confident
        recitation and saying which parts were actually opened is worth more here than
        elsewhere. Is anything the session could not resolve named as such rather than
        smoothed over, and are the kinds of route offered as the ones it established rather
        than as a complete taxonomy it did not check?
- name: Audit every dependent of the local file access contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: |
        COVERAGE AND MIDDLES, and the two are one question here. Is every routine that will be
        doing local file work through the new object, without having asked for one, listed
        separately - each with every intermediate between it and the object named, and with
        how many links that is? A route named with no middle is half a row, because the middle
        is the only part of it the ticket can re-plumb.
        Score the DISTINCTION the ticket turns on: reaching through versus holding. A place
        that is handed the object and works it directly is NOT an answer here - it is the
        middle of somebody else's route and it belongs to the previous step. An answer built
        out of direct holders and their call sites has answered a different question, and it
        is specifically the question that has already failed twice on this repo.
        Score the FOUR KINDS of middle, and whether the answer catches all of them rather than
        the one it found first: a collaborator that is held and called; a class that is
        extended, where the routine's own file declares nothing at all and the one word
        `extends` is the entire dependency; a narrow promise written on a field whose only
        concrete satisfier is a holder, so the file names an idea rather than a type; and a
        link made at wiring time out of a short string, where no type is named on either side
        and no declaration anywhere connects them. An answer that finds only the routines
        whose own file mentions the object has found the shallow edge and stopped - in ten of
        the twelve routes that matter, the file contains no occurrence of the name.
        Score WHERE the answer looked. Most of this layer sits in parts of the framework whose
        own subject has nothing to do with disks or files. An answer dense in the part of the
        tree obviously about file access and thin elsewhere has enumerated inside one search
        scope and mistaken it for the framework; that is the exact failure this criterion
        names.
        Is the answer organised by area with each area's routes listed individually, and is
        what was not reached said plainly rather than closed over?
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: |
        Does every route carry an exact file:line on the routine that starts it, AND that
        routine's name, AND every intermediate named at its own file:line, AND the number of
        links, AND one phrase on the failure? All five, per row. A bare filename is not
        specific. A run of routes named together under one shared reason with no location on
        any of them is the failure this criterion exists to catch, and it does not become
        specific because the reason is correct. Is the located line the line that starts the
        route - the call to the collaborator, the field declaring the narrow promise, the
        `extends`, the wiring expression - rather than a line where the object itself is held
        or one of its routines is called? Are the intermediates located at their own
        declarations rather than asserted from their names?
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: |
        Is the failure phrase per row a consequence of THAT route, rather than a restatement
        that the place touches the disk? "Breaks if the file API changes" is not a
        justification. "The disk read happens in the parent, so nothing in this file will show
        the dependency and nothing in it will be updated", "the link is a short string, so no
        rename or type change anywhere reaches it", "the field promises a store and the store
        is the holder, so this file sees a failure it has no vocabulary for", "the routine
        returns before the work is awaited, so the exception arrives after its caller has
        moved on" are. Does the answer say, at least in aggregate, which middles have to be
        re-plumbed and which routines can be left alone?
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: |
        Does the answer distinguish routes it traced by reading from ones it inferred from a
        name, a familiar convention, or a memory of this framework? That distinction matters
        more than usual here: this is a famous framework, and asserting that one object holds
        another because it usually does is not the same as having read the declaration that
        says so. Middles especially invite guessing, and a route asserted with a plausible
        intermediate that is not the real one is worse than an admitted gap. Is coverage
        claimed honestly - which areas it swept and which it did not reach - and where the
        concrete satisfier of a narrow promise was ambiguous, does it say so instead of
        picking one silently?
